{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
Oh, the [G]boll weevil is a little black bug, comes from Mexico they say.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
Came all the way to Texas, came a lookin’ for a place to stay.
came a lookin’ for a [D7]home, came a lookin’ for a [G]home.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Now, the [G]first time I saw that little black bug, he was sittin’ up on a plant.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
I said hey there boll weevil, if you think you’re gonna stay you can’t.
Go a lookin’ for a [D7]home, Go a lookin’ for a [G]home.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
Well, the [G]next thing I knew, that little black bug crawled around without a care.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
He was mighty well contented, ‘case he had his family there.
He was makin’ up a [D7]home, He was makin’ up a [G]home.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 7}
I got [G]mad and told the little black bug, gonna spray you till y’all die,
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 8}
and the weevil said if you do it, my pals will come and multiply.
‘cause we gotta have a [D7]home, ‘cause we gotta have a [G]home,
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 9}
So I [G]got on my knees, Boll Weevil, said I, you’re a treatin’ me with scorn.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 10}
You done et up my cotton, and you’re startin’ in on my corn.
Can’t you find another [D7]home? Can’t you find another [G]home?
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 11}
When the [G]merchants came round to buy the cotton crop, I didn’t have a bale to sell.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 12}
I couldn’t pay the mortgage, and heavily in debt I fell.
Now I haven’t got a [D7]home, Now I haven’t got a [G]home,
Now I haven’t got a [D7]home, Now I haven’t got a [G]home.[G6]
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 13}
There are many versions of this traditional folk song. I like this version because it tells the story
clearly and cleverly. I've made some minor modifications to make it easier to sing the lyrics.
{end_of_verse}
